\begin{abstract}
This hybrid scientific--engineering white sheet presents a closed-loop hydrodynamic control fabric designed to transform catastrophic flooding into a continuous, regulated flow regime. The system integrates multi-tier basin architecture, permanent spillways, reversible pump networks, variable-height labyrinth weirs, and real-time autonomous intelligence looping. Scientific foundations include shallow-water dynamics, Lyapunov stability analysis, overshoot bounds, and safety invariants. Engineering foundations include cyber-physical telemetry ingestion, FPGA/GPU accelerated solvers, constraint optimization engines, and hardware-in-the-loop deployment. A three-phase validation pathway is proposed: formal mathematical proofs, high-fidelity historical storm replay simulations, and pilot basin deployment. The hybrid approach provides a unified doctrine for planetary-scale flood mitigation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Scientific Motivation}
Flooding is a nonlinear hydrodynamic failure mode arising from trapped potential, insufficient spillway logic, and reactive basin management. Traditional infrastructure lacks predictive control, resulting in overtopping, levee failure, and catastrophic surge.

\subsection{Engineering Motivation}
Modern cyber-physical systems enable real-time telemetry ingestion, accelerated physics solvers, and autonomous actuation. These capabilities allow hydrodynamic systems to operate as continuous flow regulators rather than static reservoirs.

\subsection{Niagara-Logic Principle}
Niagara Falls provides a natural model of continuous spillway behavior. This work generalizes that principle into engineered multi-tier basin architectures capable of autonomous regulation.

